{
  "concept": "As the angle x approaches a fixed angle c, the point P(cos x, sin x) on the unit circle approaches Q(cos c, sin c), so cos x → cos c and sin x → sin c — i.e. sine and cosine are continuous everywhere.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrowhead (right) and vertical y-axis with arrowhead (up), thin black",
    "Unit circle drawn as a thick blue arc centered at origin, with small dashed gaps in the upper-left and lower-left to suggest it continues",
    "Origin O at the axis intersection (unlabeled dot or vertex of the angles)",
    "Red radius segment from origin to point P on the circle",
    "Black radius segment from origin to point Q on the circle",
    "Shaded sector (light purple/magenta gradient) bounded by the two radii and the arc between P and Q",
    "Red filled dot at P with label 'P(cos x, sin x)' to the right of the circle",
    "Blue filled dot at Q with label 'Q(cos c, sin c)' above-right of the circle",
    "Small arc with label 'x' near the origin marking the angle from the positive x-axis to OP",
    "Larger arc with label 'c' marking the angle from the positive x-axis to OQ",
    "Curved arrow outside the circle pointing from near P toward Q indicating P's motion",
    "Caption box with gray border at bottom: 'As x approaches c the point P approaches the point Q.'",
    "Readout panel showing current numeric values of x, c, cos x, cos c, sin x, sin c and the gap |x − c|",
    "Optional dashed guide lines from P and Q to the axes showing their cosine (horizontal) and sine (vertical) coordinates"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angleX",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle x (radians)",
      "range": [0, 3.14159, 0.005],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "Dragging x moves P along the circle; as x gets numerically close to c, both coordinates of P get close to those of Q and the shaded sector collapses."
    },
    {
      "id": "angleC",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Target angle c (radians)",
      "range": [0.2, 3.0, 0.01],
      "default": 1.15,
      "teaches": "The limit statement holds for every choice of c — relocating Q shows continuity is not special to one point."
    },
    {
      "id": "approach",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Approach c",
      "states": ["idle", "from left", "from right", "both sides"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "Both one-sided approaches drive P to the same point Q, so the two-sided limit exists and equals the function value at c."
    },
    {
      "id": "showCoords",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show coordinate projections",
      "options": ["off", "on"],
      "default": "off",
      "teaches": "The horizontal and vertical drop-lines make visible that P→Q is exactly the pair of statements cos x → cos c and sin x → sin c."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The setup",
      "narration": "Q sits at the fixed angle c on the unit circle; P sits at the variable angle x. The shaded sector between the two radii measures how far apart they are.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.15, "angleX": 0.5, "approach": "idle", "showCoords": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide x toward c",
      "narration": "Drag x upward and watch P climb the arc toward Q — the sector narrows and the arc between the points shortens.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.15, "angleX": 1.0, "approach": "idle", "showCoords": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading the coordinates",
      "narration": "Turn on the projections: P's shadow on the x-axis is cos x and on the y-axis is sin x. As P nears Q, each shadow slides toward cos c and sin c.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.15, "angleX": 1.08, "approach": "idle", "showCoords": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approach from the left",
      "narration": "Let x increase up to c. P creeps along the arc and the sector shrinks to nothing — cos x → cos c and sin x → sin c from below.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.15, "angleX": 1.14, "approach": "from left", "showCoords": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approach from the right",
      "narration": "Now start past c and come back down. P arrives at the same point Q, so the one-sided limits agree.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.15, "angleX": 1.6, "approach": "from right", "showCoords": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Any c at all",
      "narration": "Move Q to a different angle and repeat: the same collapse happens. Since c was arbitrary, sine and cosine are continuous at every real number.",
      "state": { "angleC": 2.4, "angleX": 1.8, "approach": "both sides", "showCoords": "on" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Continuity of sine and cosine is nothing more than the geometric fact that nearby angles put you at nearby points on the circle — P→Q is literally the statement cos x → cos c and sin x → sin c.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Portrait layout. A thick sky-blue circle of radius ~1 centered on plain black axes with arrowheads; the circle is drawn mostly complete but with two short dashed breaks on its left side (upper-left and lower-left) as in the textbook. Two radii leave the origin into the first/second quadrant: a red segment to P (red dot, angle x, lower) and a black segment to Q (blue dot, angle c, higher). The wedge between them is filled with a soft purple-to-white gradient. Angle x is marked by a small arc with an arrowhead near the origin labeled italic 'x'; angle c is marked by a larger concentric arc labeled italic 'c' inside the wedge. Labels 'P(cos x, sin x)' in black sit to the right of the red dot, 'Q(cos c, sin c)' above-right of the blue dot; a curved black arrow sweeps from just outside P up to Q. A light-gray-bordered caption box below the figure reads 'As x approaches c the point P approaches the point Q.' Add a small numeric readout (x, c, |x−c|, cos x vs cos c, sin x vs sin c) beside or under the caption. Optional dashed gray projection lines from P and Q down to the x-axis and across to the y-axis when the coordinate toggle is on, with red/blue tick marks converging as x→c."
}

